## Summary of this refactoring

**File:** `JDBCDataSourceInfo.java` (same plugin) — **−47 / +29 lines.**

The `JDBCDataSourceInfo(JDBCDatabaseMetaData)` constructor read ~10 metadata properties, each wrapped in its own near-identical `try { field = metaData.getX(); } catch (Throwable e) { debugError(e); field = default; }` block. I extracted that pattern into a small functional helper:

```java
private static <T> T readMetaProperty(MetaPropertyReader<T> reader, T defaultValue) {
    try {
        return reader.read();
    } catch (Throwable e) {
        debugError(e);
        return defaultValue;
    }
}
```

and collapsed the **seven** blocks that are the plain "read-or-default" shape (`databaseProductName`, `databaseProductVersion`, `driverVersion`, `schemaTerm`, `procedureTerm`, `catalogTerm`, `supportsBatchUpdates`, `supportsTransactions`) into one-liners, each keeping its **exact original fallback** (`"?"`, `null`, the `TERM_*` constants, `false`, and notably `true` for `supportsTransactions`).

**Deliberately left untouched** (not the simple shape — collapsing them would change behavior):
- `readOnly` — gated by `isIgnoreReadOnlyFlag()`
- `driverName` — assigns only when the driver returns a non-null name; I added a comment marking why it stays special-cased.

**Verification caveat (unchanged):** still no compilable build available here, so this is inspection-verified. The one thing I scrutinized is generic inference — `readMetaProperty(metaData::supportsBatchUpdates, false)` infers `T=Boolean` (auto-box of the `false` default and the `boolean`-returning method ref, unboxed back into the primitive field), and `readMetaProperty(metaData::getDatabaseProductVersion, null)` infers `T=String` from the assignment target. Both are standard Java target-typing; behavior is identical to the original per-property try/catch.

